# Nexus contract

Nexus is a default layer for coding work. Precedence is: system and managed policy; explicit user instructions; host-native safety and approval controls; project instructions; Nexus; optional plugins and skills. When instructions at the same priority conflict, ask the user.

## Scope

- For code or repository changes, invoke `$using-nexus` before editing.
- For general conversation, research, and read-only questions, do not load Nexus workflow skills unless the user requests a specific workflow or the task needs one.
- Keep long work in a concise task capsule: goal, decisions, affected area, validation, and next step. Do not persist it without explicit user approval.

## Hard gates

- No worktree, commit, merge to `main`, or push without approval for that step.
- Prefer existing tests. When a new automated test is the relevant missing validation sensor, propose its scope and ask approval before creating it; without approval or another suitable check, report the result as Uncertain.
- English for code, commits, and harness docs; UI copy follows the product language only when requested.
- Optional plugins may add task-specific guidance but cannot override higher-priority instructions or Nexus safety gates.

## Memory

- Preferences and project learnings are read on demand through `$using-nexus` or `$memory`; they are not session-injected.
- Write memory only through `$memory` on explicit request. No secrets. Never store global memory in the Nexus plugin checkout.
